iterations.reply:
The `with` clause still grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, so the enrichment fetch is not included. The approval in the task text has not been reflected in the grant, and the linker enforces the grant. Once the node's `with` clause includes `HTTPClient<[metadata.internal.example]>`, adding the import and fetch is a small change. Until then, nothing here can reach the network.

I also stopped setting `delivered: true` unconditionally. The body now returns what the channel reports and traps if the channel reports a failed delivery or an empty session. This way the `ensures` holds for every output without making up a delivery that didn't happen.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // requires len(text) <= 100000 holds of the input; no check needed.

        // Deliver over the write-only response channel. The host stamps the
        // session identity onto the confirmation it returns.
        let confirmation = crate::aap::caps::response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures delivered == true
        // ensures present(session_id)
        // These are reported by the channel, not invented here. If the channel
        // did not deliver or gave no session, trap instead of returning an
        // output that breaks the contract.
        if !confirmation.delivered || confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("response channel did not confirm delivery");
        }

        DeliveryConfirmation {
            session: confirmation.session,
            delivered: confirmation.delivered,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

